<!-- ===== SECTION 1: FILE BASICS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="file-basics">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Understanding File Operations</h2>

  <p>Every AI model eventually needs to save its trained weights. Every dataset you work with comes from somewhere — likely a file. Every pipeline needs to log its outputs. File I/O is how you bridge the gap between transient memory and persistent storage. Unlike variables that disappear when your Python program exits, files live on disk, surviving restarts and sharing data across machines.</p>

  <p>Python abstracts file operations behind a simple interface: open a file, read from it or write to it, and close it. But understanding what happens behind those abstractions is crucial for writing efficient, error-resistant code. Today's lesson covers all the modes, encodings, and techniques professionals use in production systems.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A file is like a library book. Before you can read it, you must check it out (open). While you have it checked out, others might not access it. When you are done, you must return it (close). If you never return it, the library cannot lend it to others. In Python, if you never close a file, your operating system limits how many you can have open at once, and other programs might not see your changes.</p>
  </div>

