  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Bad: Manual file handling (can leak resources)</span>
<span class="va">f</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"data.txt"</span>, <span class="st">"r"</span>)
<span class="va">content</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">read</span>()
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">content</span>)
<span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">close</span>()  <span class="cm"># Easy to forget!</span>

<span class="cm"># Good: Context manager (automatic closing)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"data.txt"</span>, <span class="st">"r"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">content</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">read</span>()
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">content</span>)
<span class="cm"># File automatically closed here, even if error occurred</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> The first example manually closes the file. If an error occurs between read and close, the file never closes. The second uses a context manager (with statement), which guarantees the file closes, even if an exception is raised. The context manager is the Pythonic way and should be your default.</p>
  </div>

