<!-- ===== SECTION 2: TEXT FILES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="text-files">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Reading and Writing Text Files</h2>

  <p>Most data pipelines work with text files: CSV datasets, JSON configurations, log files, and plain text documents. Python treats text as a sequence of characters, automatically handling encoding conversions (usually UTF-8). Understanding text file operations is foundational for data science work.</p>

  <h3>Reading Text Files</h3>
  <p>Python provides three main methods to read text: read() (entire file), readline() (one line), and readlines() (list of lines). For large files, line-by-line iteration is most memory-efficient, avoiding loading multi-gigabyte files into RAM.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Reading entire file (OK for small files)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">content</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">read</span>()  <span class="cm"># Returns one giant string</span>

<span class="cm"># Line-by-line iteration (memory efficient)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">line</span> <span class="op">in</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">line</span>.<span class="fn">strip</span>())  <span class="cm"># Remove newline</span>

<span class="cm"># Get all lines as a list</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">lines</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">readlines</span>()  <span class="cm"># List[str]</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> The first approach loads the entire file into memory as a single string. The second iterates line by line, reading only the current line into memory — crucial for processing massive logs or datasets. The third gets all lines as a list, useful when you need random access but still reasonable file sizes.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Writing and Appending Text</h3>
  <p>Mode 'w' overwrites files completely. Mode 'a' appends to the end without destroying existing content. Always be cautious with 'w' — it silently deletes the old file.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Writing (overwrites if exists)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="st">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="st">"Line 1\n"</span>)
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="st">"Line 2\n"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Appending (keeps existing content)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="st">"a"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="st">"Line 3\n"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Writing multiple lines at once</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="st">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">lines</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">"A\n"</span>, <span class="st">"B\n"</span>, <span class="st">"C\n"</span>]
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">writelines</span>(<span class="va">lines</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> write() appends a single string (remember to include \n for newlines). writelines() takes a list of strings and writes them sequentially. In 'a' mode, new content goes to the end without removing existing data.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Transaction logs must be appended without data loss. Using write mode instead of append mode would corrupt your audit trail. Log files grow continuously, and you must handle encoding for international characters in currency notation.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Medical datasets are often in plain text formats (like FASTA for genetic sequences). Understanding line-by-line reading allows processing datasets larger than system RAM. Encoding matters when reading non-ASCII characters in patient names or international research data.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>read() loads entire file — use for small files only</li>
      <li>Iterate directly with for loop for memory-efficient line-by-line reading</li>
      <li>write() overwrites; append() adds to the end</li>
      <li>Remember to include \n in write() calls for newlines</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- QUIZ -->
  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>Which reading method is most memory-efficient for a 10 GB log file?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) f.read() — loads entire file at once</li>
        <li>B) for line in f: — processes one line at a time</li>
        <li>C) f.readlines() — loads all lines into a list</li>
        <li>D) All methods use the same memory</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">The for loop iterates line by line, keeping only the current line in memory. read() and readlines() load the entire file, which would exhaust RAM for a 10 GB file.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

