<!-- ===== SECTION 3: SERIALIZATION ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="serialization">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
  <h2>Serialization: Pickle and JSON</h2>

  <p>Plain text files store strings and numbers, but what about complex Python objects? When you train a machine learning model, you need to save the weights, biases, and configuration — not as text, but as actual Python dictionaries and arrays. Serialization transforms Python objects into bytes that can be written to disk and later reconstructed. The two main approaches are pickle (Python-specific, handles most objects) and JSON (human-readable, language-independent).</p>

  <h3>Pickle: Python's Native Serialization</h3>
  <p>Pickle converts any Python object to a byte stream. Unmarshal it later and you get back the exact object. It is powerful but has security implications: never unpickle data from untrusted sources, as malicious pickle data can execute arbitrary code.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">pickle</span>

<span class="cm"># Complex object: dictionary with lists</span>
<span class="va">model_config</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"name"</span>: <span class="st">"ResNet50"</span>,
    <span class="st">"layers"</span>: [<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="nm">256</span>, <span class="nm">512</span>],
    <span class="st">"trained"</span>: <span class="kw">True</span>
}

<span class="cm"># Save to pickle file</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"model.pkl"</span>, <span class="st">"wb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="nm">pickle</span>.<span class="fn">dump</span>(<span class="va">model_config</span>, <span class="va">f</span>)

<span class="cm"># Load from pickle file</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"model.pkl"</span>, <span class="st">"rb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">loaded</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">pickle</span>.<span class="fn">load</span>(<span class="va">f</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">loaded</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> pickle.dump() serializes the object and writes bytes to the file (notice 'wb' mode). pickle.load() reads the bytes and reconstructs the object. The object is identical — same values, same nested structure. But only Python can read pickle files.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>JSON: Human-Readable, Language-Independent</h3>
  <p>JSON is text-based and human-readable. It works across Python, JavaScript, Java, and nearly every programming language. Downside: it only handles basic types (strings, numbers, booleans, lists, dicts). Custom objects must be converted.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">json</span>

<span class="va">config</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"learning_rate"</span>: <span class="nm">0.001</span>,
    <span class="st">"epochs"</span>: <span class="nm">50</span>,
    <span class="st">"batch_size"</span>: <span class="nm">32</span>,
    <span class="st">"layers"</span>: [<span class="nm">256</span>, <span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="nm">64</span>]
}

<span class="cm"># Save to JSON</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"config.json"</span>, <span class="st">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="nm">json</span>.<span class="fn">dump</span>(<span class="va">config</span>, <span class="va">f</span>, <span class="va">indent</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>)

<span class="cm"># Load from JSON</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"config.json"</span>, <span class="st">"r"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">loaded_config</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">json</span>.<span class="fn">load</span>(<span class="va">f</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">loaded_config</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> json.dump() writes a text file with indentation (indent=2 makes it readable). The file is text, so you can open it in any editor. json.load() parses the text back to Python objects. JSON is the standard for configuration files in production systems.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- INFOGRAPHIC: Pickle vs JSON -->
  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Pickle vs JSON: When to Use Each</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>🔒 Pickle</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Handles any Python object (custom classes)</li>
          <li>Binary format (compact, fast)</li>
          <li>Python-only (not language-independent)</li>
          <li>Security risk with untrusted data</li>
          <li>Use for: model weights, cached objects</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>📄 JSON</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Text-based, human-readable</li>
          <li>Larger file sizes than pickle</li>
          <li>Works across all languages</li>
          <li>Safe from untrusted sources</li>
          <li>Use for: configuration, APIs, logs</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Pickle is like taking a Polaroid photo of your Python object, then laminating it into bytes. Only another Python interpreter can develop the photo and see the original object. JSON is like writing a detailed description of the object in plain English that anyone (programmer in any language) can read and reconstruct. You lose some nuance but gain universal compatibility.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ===== SECTION 4: BINARY FILES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="binary-files">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Binary Files and Efficient I/O</h2>

  <p>Not all data is text. Images, audio, trained models, and sensor data are stored as binary — raw bytes without character encoding. Working with binary files requires opening files in binary mode ('rb', 'wb') and understanding how data is actually stored on disk.</p>

  <h3>Reading and Writing Binary Data</h3>
  <p>Binary mode reads/writes bytes instead of strings. This is essential for handling numpy arrays, images, and audio directly to disk without encoding overhead.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="nm">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Create a numpy array</span>
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">1.5</span>, <span class="nm">2.7</span>, <span class="nm">3.2</span>, <span class="nm">4.1</span>])

<span class="cm"># Save directly as binary (most efficient)</span>
<span class="va">data</span>.<span class="fn">tofile</span>(<span class="st">"array.bin"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Or use pickle for numpy arrays</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">pickle</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"array.pkl"</span>, <span class="st">"wb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="nm">pickle</span>.<span class="fn">dump</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="va">f</span>)

<span class="cm"># Load back the array</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"array.pkl"</span>, <span class="st">"rb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">loaded</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">pickle</span>.<span class="fn">load</span>(<span class="va">f</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> tofile() writes numpy array bytes directly (fastest). Pickle works too and preserves shape/dtype metadata. For large datasets in AI pipelines, binary I/O eliminates text encoding overhead, significantly speeding up disk operations.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Binary mode ('rb', 'wb') works with bytes, not strings</li>
      <li>Use pickle for complex Python objects including numpy arrays</li>
      <li>Use tofile() for raw numpy array serialization (fastest)</li>
      <li>JSON for configuration and data interchange across languages</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ===== SECTION 5: FILE PATTERNS ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="file-patterns">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Production File I/O Patterns</h2>

  <p>In production AI systems, file I/O must be robust. What if the disk fills up mid-write? What if the network drops while reading from a cloud storage service? Professional code handles these edge cases with proven patterns.</p>

  <h3>Safe Writing with Temporary Files</h3>
  <p>Never write directly to the final destination. Write to a temporary file first, then atomically move it. This prevents corruption if the process crashes.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">tempfile</span>, <span class="nm">os</span>

<span class="va">final_path</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"model_weights.pkl"</span>

<span class="cm"># Write to temporary file, then move atomically</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">tempfile</span>.<span class="fn">NamedTemporaryFile</span>(
    <span class="va">mode</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"wb"</span>, <span class="va">delete</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>
) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">tmp</span>:
    <span class="va">tmp_path</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tmp</span>.<span class="va">name</span>
    <span class="cm"># Write to temporary file</span>
    <span class="nm">pickle</span>.<span class="fn">dump</span>(<span class="va">weights</span>, <span class="va">tmp</span>)

<span class="cm"># Move is atomic on most systems</span>
<span class="nm">os</span>.<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="va">tmp_path</span>, <span class="va">final_path</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> A temporary file is created, data is written to it, then it is atomically moved to the final location. If the program crashes during write, the corrupt temporary file is abandoned; the original file remains untouched. This pattern is standard in production systems.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Buffering and Performance</h3>
  <p>Python buffers I/O for performance. For critical operations, force a flush to ensure data hits disk immediately.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Unbuffered writes for critical data</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"critical.log"</span>, <span class="st">"w"</span>, <span class="va">buffering</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="st">"Important event\n"</span>)
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">flush</span>()  <span class="cm"># Immediately write to disk</span>

<span class="cm"># Or use line buffering (flush at each newline)</span>
<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"output.log"</span>, <span class="st">"w"</span>, <span class="va">buffering</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
    <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="st">"Event A\n"</span>)  <span class="cm"># Flushes automatically</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> By default, Python buffers writes for efficiency. For critical data (transaction logs, model checkpoints), buffering=1 enables line buffering (flushes at each newline), or you can manually call flush(). This ensures data persists immediately, protecting against unexpected crashes.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Financial AI:</strong> Trading systems log every transaction. A crash mid-write could corrupt records or lose audit trails. Atomic writes (write-then-move) and line buffering ensure every transaction is safely recorded, protecting both compliance and debugging.</p>
    <p><strong>In Life Sciences AI:</strong> Research data is irreplaceable. Using atomic writes ensures partial results never overwrite complete experiments. Batch processing large datasets with proper buffering and error handling prevents data loss when processing million-record medical datasets.</p>
  </div>
</section>

